/// \defgroup grp_w1018_gui_checkbox_radio_slider_draw Gadget drawing: check box, radio button, horizontal slider and progress bar
/// \ingroup grp_gui_apt
/// \brief The W3D device layer's draw callbacks for check boxes, radio
///        buttons, horizontal sliders and progress bars, plus the check
///        box's input and system callbacks and its two setters.
///
/// A gadget is a GameWindow with a gadget style and a block of
/// gadget-specific data (see \ref grp_w1_gui_gadgets). Its behaviour lives
/// in the game engine (GadgetCheckBox.cpp and its siblings), but its
/// drawing lives in the W3D device layer, in one W3D source file per
/// gadget family. Each family has two looks:
/// - a \e colour look, drawn from rectangles and lines in the colours of
///   the window's draw data (W3DGadgetCheckBoxDraw(),
///   W3DGadgetRadioButtonDraw(), W3DGadgetHorizontalSliderDraw(),
///   W3DGadgetProgressBarDraw());
/// - an \e image look, drawn from the images of the same draw data
///   (W3DGadgetCheckBoxImageDraw(), W3DGadgetRadioButtonImageDraw(),
///   W3DGadgetHorizontalSliderImageDraw()).
///
/// \par How a draw callback is reached
/// In BFME 2 each look is a small GameWindow subclass whose virtual draw
/// method forwards the window and its instance data to one of these
/// functions and returns 1. The window classes of both check box looks,
/// colour and image, forward their virtual input and system methods to
/// GadgetCheckBoxInput() and GadgetCheckBoxSystem(): retail's two check box
/// window vtables share those two slots, and only the draw slot differs by
/// look. The colour-look horizontal and vertical sliders likewise share one
/// draw method, which calls W3DGadgetHorizontalSliderDraw(); only their
/// input and system methods differ. The W3D window manager decides the look once,
/// when the gadget is created: its gadget-creation overrides test the
/// window's image status flag, store one of two window-creation functions,
/// one per look, in the creation record, and then pass that record to the
/// window manager's generic factory for the gadget type. Zero Hour's
/// counterpart for a check box is GameWindowManager::gogoGadgetCheckbox,
/// which takes the parent, status, position, size, instance data and font
/// as separate arguments instead of a record. That these creation
/// functions build the per-look subclasses, and that the subclasses exist
/// at all, is inferred from the forwarding bodies in the
/// W3D gadget sources; the classes' own names are not known. The draw
/// function names are target facts: retail's function lexicon lists each
/// one by name. Zero Hour: the factory stores the callbacks as function
/// pointers on a plain GameWindow, choosing the draw function by the IMAGE
/// status flag through W3DGameWindowManager's getCheckBoxDrawFunc,
/// getCheckBoxImageDrawFunc and their siblings.
///
/// \par Common drawing model
/// - The visual state is chosen from two places: the window's status (the
///   enabled flag) and its instance state (the highlighted and selected
///   bits). Disabled wins over highlighted, which wins over plain enabled;
///   for check boxes and radio buttons the selected bit then picks the
///   checked or unchecked variant. The image-look radio button is the
///   exception: selected wins over everything (see
///   W3DGadgetRadioButtonImageDraw()).
/// - Colours and images come from the window's three draw-data arrays,
///   one per state (enabled, disabled, highlighted), each slot holding an
///   image, a colour and a border colour. Which slot means what is a
///   per-gadget convention fixed by the inline getters of Zero Hour's
///   gadget headers (GadgetCheckBox.h, GadgetRadioButton.h,
///   GadgetSlider.h, GadgetProgressBar.h); for example, a check box's first
///   slot is its background and the next two are the unchecked and checked
///   box. Some elements are skipped when their colour is the
///   undefined-colour value (the check box's cross, the slider's and the
///   progress bar's rectangles); the others are drawn whatever the colour.
/// - All primitives go through the window manager (winOpenRect,
///   winFillRect, winDrawLine, winDrawImage on TheWindowManager), text goes
///   through the window's DisplayString, and the image-look radio button
///   and slider also ask TheDisplay for clipping or the screen size.
///   Coordinates are screen pixels: the window's screen position and size
///   are read at the top of every call.
/// - Text: the check box and radio button helpers pick the text colour and
///   its drop-shadow colour by the same state rule, give the display
///   string the window's font if it differs, measure it, and draw it with
///   a one-pixel shadow offset. BFME 2's DisplayString interface differs
///   from Zero Hour's here: the colours are set by a separate call before
///   drawing, and the draw call takes the shadow offset. The result on
///   screen is the same as Zero Hour's default one-pixel offset.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The bodies are Zero Hour's W3DCheckBox.cpp, W3DRadioButton.cpp,
/// W3DHorizontalSlider.cpp, W3DProgressBar.cpp and GadgetCheckBox.cpp,
/// reached through the BFME 1 reconstruction. The functions documented
/// here were compared with both the Zero Hour source and the matched BFME 1
/// bodies; the differences are noted on each function. Code-shaping changes
/// that do not alter behaviour (local coordinate types, interface views)
/// are not described.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Everything here is client-side GUI. None of it reads or writes
///   game-logic state, so changing it cannot desynchronise a multiplayer
///   game or affect saves and replays. The check box only reports clicks to
///   its owner window; what a click changes is decided by the screen that
///   owns it.
/// - Data-driven: the look (the image status flag, IMAGE in a layout's
///   STATUS list), every colour and image (the layout's ENABLEDDRAWDATA,
///   DISABLEDDRAWDATA and HILITEDRAWDATA blocks, see parseDrawData()), the
///   image offset (IMAGEOFFSET), the window's size and position, its font
///   and its text label.
/// - Hard-coded: all geometry (proportions, insets, gaps), the progress
///   bar's white and light grey bevel lines, the slider's 800x600
///   reference resolution, and the three check box sound event names (see
///   GadgetCheckBoxInput()).
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - This is the seam between the window system and the renderer. A new
///   renderer can keep these functions as they are if it implements the
///   window manager's rectangle, line and image primitives, the
///   DisplayString text calls and TheDisplay's clipping and screen-size
///   calls; or it can replace them one look at a time. The colour-look
///   slider draw is the exception: W3DGadgetHorizontalSliderDraw() serves
///   both slider orientations, so replacing it changes vertical sliders
///   too.
/// - Only W3DGadgetHorizontalSliderImageDraw() scales for resolution, by
///   separate width and height factors relative to 800x600. The other draw
///   functions take their sizes from the window's own size and never scale
///   text. The exception is W3DGadgetRadioButtonImageDraw(): its caps and
///   middle tiles keep their images' native pixel widths, and only their
///   height and the number of middle tiles follow the window, so enlarging
///   the window adds tiles but leaves the caps and tiles unscaled. Beyond
///   that, resolution independence has to come from how windows and fonts
///   are sized when a layout is loaded (see \ref sub_gui_apt_remastering).
/// - When BFME 2's interfaces differ from Zero Hour's (DisplayString's text
///   colour call, Display's clipping calls), the matched code uses BFME 2's.
///   Code ported from Zero Hour cannot assume Zero Hour's interface layout.
///
/// \see \ref sub_gui_apt, \ref grp_w1_gui_gadgets,
///      \ref grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists, \ref grp_w2_gui_window_text


/// \file Gadget/W3DCheckBox.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_gui_checkbox_radio_slider_draw
/// \brief W3D device layer: the check box's colour-look and image-look draw
///        callbacks and their shared text helper.


/// \fn static void drawCheckBoxText(GameWindow *window, WinInstanceData *instData)
/// \brief Draws a check box's label to the right of its box, vertically
///        centred, in the colour for the current state.
///
/// File-static helper of W3DGadgetCheckBoxDraw() and
/// W3DGadgetCheckBoxImageDraw(), which call it last, and only when the
/// window has text. Retail shows both callers ending in a call to this
/// helper; its name is Zero Hour's.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Returns at once when the window has no display string or the string
///   is empty.
/// - Colours: the window's disabled text and text-border colours when the
///   window is disabled, the highlighted pair when the highlighted bit is
///   set, otherwise the enabled pair. The border colour is used for the
///   drop shadow.
/// - The display string takes the window's font if it has a different one.
/// - The text starts one window height to the right of the window's left
///   edge, which leaves a square the size of the window's height for the
///   box, and is centred vertically on the window.
/// - BFME 2 sets the text and shadow colours with their own DisplayString
///   call and then draws with a one-pixel shadow offset; Zero Hour passes
///   the colours to the draw call, which uses the same offset.
///
/// \param window   The check box window.
/// \param instData The window's instance data, which holds the display
///                 string and the state bits.
///
/// \see drawRadioButtonText()


/// \fn void W3DGadgetCheckBoxDraw(GameWindow *window, WinInstanceData *instData)
/// \brief Draw callback for colour-look check boxes: a bordered background,
///        a small square box with a cross, and the label.
///
/// The colour-look check box's window class calls this from its virtual
/// draw method (see the group description). The retail function lexicon
/// names it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Picks four colours (background, background border, box, box border):
///   the state rule in the group description picks the disabled,
///   highlighted or enabled draw data, and the selected bit then chooses
///   between that state's checked and unchecked box colours.
/// - Draws a border around the whole window in the background border
///   colour and fills the inside, one pixel in, with the background
///   colour.
/// - The box is a square one third of the window's height on a side, one
///   sixteenth of the window's width from its left edge and one third of
///   its height from its top. Its outline uses the box border colour.
/// - If the box colour is defined, two diagonal lines in that colour draw a
///   cross through the box. The checked and unchecked states therefore
///   differ only by their colours: an unchecked box shows no cross only if
///   its box colour is left undefined.
/// - Draws the label with drawCheckBoxText() if the window has text.
///
/// The body is Zero Hour's; the matched BFME 2 and BFME 1 bodies differ
/// from it only in code shape and in the text helper's DisplayString calls.
///
/// \param window   The check box window.
/// \param instData Its instance data.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The colours are the window's draw-data colours: first slot for the
/// background, second for the unchecked box, third for the checked box,
/// each with its border colour, in each of the enabled, disabled and
/// highlighted arrays (roles from Zero Hour's GadgetCheckBox.h getters).
/// The proportions are fixed in code.
///
/// \see W3DGadgetCheckBoxImageDraw(), GadgetCheckBoxInput()


/// \fn void W3DGadgetCheckBoxImageDraw(GameWindow *window, WinInstanceData *instData)
/// \brief Draw callback for image-look check boxes: one box image for the
///        current state, then the label.
///
/// The image-look check box's window class calls this from its virtual
/// draw method. The retail function lexicon names it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Picks the box image by the same state rule as the colour look:
///   disabled, highlighted or enabled, then the checked or unchecked image
///   by the selected bit.
/// - Draws no background image. Zero Hour's code for one is commented out,
///   and the BFME 2 body has none either, so a background must come from
///   another window behind the check box.
/// - If there is a box image, it is drawn as a square whose side is the
///   window's height less six pixels, three pixels below the window's top
///   and starting at the window's left edge plus the horizontal image
///   offset. The vertical image offset is not used.
/// - Draws the label with drawCheckBoxText() if the window has text.
///
/// \param window   The check box window.
/// \param instData Its instance data; also supplies the image offset.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The images are the second (unchecked) and third (checked) image slots
/// of the enabled, disabled and highlighted draw data. A missing image
/// simply draws nothing for that state. The box image is stretched to the
/// computed square whatever its own size.
///
/// \see W3DGadgetCheckBoxDraw()


/// \file Gadget/W3DRadioButton.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_gui_checkbox_radio_slider_draw
/// \brief W3D device layer: the radio button's colour-look and image-look
///        draw callbacks and their shared text helper.


/// \fn static void drawRadioButtonText(GameWindow *window, WinInstanceData *instData)
/// \brief Draws a radio button's label centred on the button, in the colour
///        for the current state.
///
/// File-static helper of W3DGadgetRadioButtonDraw() and
/// W3DGadgetRadioButtonImageDraw(), which call it last, and only when the
/// window has text. Retail shows both callers ending in a call to this
/// helper; its name is Zero Hour's.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The same steps as drawCheckBoxText(): return if there is no text, pick
/// the text and shadow colours as disabled, highlighted or enabled, give the
/// display string the window's font, measure it, then set the colours and
/// draw with a one-pixel shadow offset. Only the position differs: the
/// text is centred both horizontally and vertically on the window.
///
/// \param window   The radio button window.
/// \param instData The window's instance data.
///
/// \see drawCheckBoxText()


/// \fn void W3DGadgetRadioButtonDraw(GameWindow *window, WinInstanceData *instData)
/// \brief Draw callback for colour-look radio buttons: a bordered bar with a
///        filled square at each end, and the label centred.
///
/// The colour-look radio button's window class calls this from its virtual
/// draw method. The retail function lexicon names it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Picks the background, background border, box and box border colours by
///   the same rule as W3DGadgetCheckBoxDraw(), using the radio button's
///   draw-data getters.
/// - Draws a border round the whole window and fills the inside, one pixel
///   in.
/// - Draws two squares as tall as the window, one at each end: for each, a
///   vertical line in the background border colour separates it from the
///   middle, and it is filled with the box colour. The box border colour is
///   chosen but never drawn.
/// - Draws the label centred with drawRadioButtonText() if the window has
///   text.
///
/// The body is Zero Hour's apart from the text helper's DisplayString
/// calls.
///
/// \param window   The radio button window.
/// \param instData Its instance data.
///
/// \see W3DGadgetRadioButtonImageDraw()


/// \fn void W3DGadgetRadioButtonImageDraw(GameWindow *window, WinInstanceData *instData)
/// \brief Draw callback for image-look radio buttons: a three-piece
///        horizontal bar (left cap, repeated middle, right cap) for the
///        current state, then the label.
///
/// The image-look radio button's window class calls this from its virtual
/// draw method. The retail function lexicon names it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - State order differs from every other function here: the selected bit
///   is tested first, so a selected button shows its selected images even
///   when disabled. Otherwise disabled, highlighted and enabled are tested
///   in the usual order.
/// - Each state supplies three images. In the enabled, disabled and
///   highlighted arrays they are the first three slots, which Zero Hour's
///   getters call the background, unchecked-box and checked-box images; this
///   function uses them as the left cap, the middle piece and the right
///   cap. The selected images are three further slots of the highlighted
///   array (Zero Hour's GadgetRadioGetSelected getters).
/// - If any of the three is missing, nothing is drawn, not even the label.
/// - The caps keep their image widths and are stretched to the window's
///   height. The middle piece is repeated from the left cap's right edge to
///   the right cap's left edge, with one more copy than fits; a clip region
///   set on TheDisplay cuts the last copy off at the right cap. Clipping is
///   then switched off before the caps are drawn.
/// - The image offset moves the left cap and the middle; the right cap
///   starts at its offset position but always ends at the window's right
///   edge. The clip region's top ignores the vertical offset.
/// - Draws the label centred with drawRadioButtonText() if the window has
///   text.
///
/// The body is Zero Hour's apart from the text helper; its clipping calls go
/// through BFME 2's Display interface, whose layout differs from Zero
/// Hour's.
///
/// \param window   The radio button window.
/// \param instData Its instance data; also supplies the image offset.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// When it draws, the function replaces the display's clip region with its
/// own and, after the middle pieces and before the caps and the label,
/// switches clipping off instead of restoring an earlier region. Any clip
/// region active before the call is therefore lost, and clipping is left
/// off afterwards. When an image is missing the function returns before
/// touching clipping.
///
/// \see W3DGadgetRadioButtonDraw()


/// \file Gadget/W3DHorizontalSlider.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_gui_checkbox_radio_slider_draw
/// \brief W3D device layer: the horizontal slider's colour-look and
///        image-look draw callbacks.
///
/// The file also holds two further slider image routines carried over
/// from Zero Hour; nothing in BFME 2 is known to call them, so they are not
/// documented here.


/// \fn void W3DGadgetHorizontalSliderDraw(GameWindow *window, WinInstanceData *instData)
/// \brief Draw callback for colour-look sliders: a bordered, filled
///        rectangle over the whole control.
///
/// The colour-look window classes of both the horizontal and the vertical
/// slider call this from their shared virtual draw method (see the group
/// description). The retail function lexicon lists this one function
/// under both the horizontal and the vertical slider's colour-draw names:
/// Zero Hour's two functions have identical bodies, and retail keeps one
/// copy.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Picks the slider's background and border colours as disabled,
/// highlighted or enabled; draws the border round the whole window if its
/// colour is defined and fills the inside, one pixel in, if the fill
/// colour is defined. It draws no thumb (see
/// W3DGadgetHorizontalSliderImageDraw()). The body is Zero Hour's.
///
/// \param window   The slider window.
/// \param instData Its instance data.
///
/// \see W3DGadgetHorizontalSliderImageDraw()


/// \fn void W3DGadgetHorizontalSliderImageDraw(GameWindow *window, WinInstanceData *instData)
/// \brief Draw callback for image-look horizontal sliders: a row of boxes,
///        the ones up to the slider's position filled, scaled from an
///        800x600 layout.
///
/// The image-look horizontal slider's window class calls this from its
/// virtual draw method. The retail function lexicon names it. This look
/// draws the slider's range as a segmented bar. Neither slider draw
/// function draws a thumb; Zero Hour's slider factory creates the thumb as
/// a separate child button window, which draws itself.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Images: the first disabled-state image is the filled box, the second
///   disabled-state image the empty box, and the first highlighted-state
///   image the highlight square. The enabled and disabled states are not
///   otherwise distinguished.
/// - Scale: unless the window has a particular status bit set (see below),
///   a horizontal factor is the display's width over 800 and a vertical
///   factor its height over 600; with the bit set both factors are 1.
/// - Box size: each box is 0.6 times the filled image's width times the
///   horizontal factor wide, and the window's height times the vertical
///   factor tall, with a one-pixel gap. The conversions to whole pixels
///   truncate.
/// - Count: boxes are laid out from the left edge while a whole box still
///   ends inside the window. A box counts as filled when it starts at or
///   before the point that is the slider's fraction of the way across the
///   window, where the fraction is the position less the minimum over the
///   maximum less the minimum. At the minimum position no box is filled.
/// - The row is then shifted right by half the unused width, which
///   centres it in the window to within a pixel or two.
/// - When the slider is highlighted, a row of highlight squares, one more
///   than the boxes, is drawn first: each a square whose side is one box
///   width plus the gap, shifted left by half that side and down by 0.8 of
///   the box height.
/// - Filled boxes are drawn, then empty ones, from the window's top.
/// - The slider's position, minimum and maximum come from its user data
///   (Zero Hour's SliderData).
///
/// \param window   The slider window.
/// \param instData Its instance data; only its highlighted bit is used.
///
/// \note Zero Hour scales only horizontally (the display's width over 800),
///       makes the boxes square at the scaled image width with a two-pixel
///       gap, and puts the highlight row a third of a box down. The two-axis
///       scale, the opt-out status bit, the 0.6 width factor, the one-pixel
///       gap and the 0.8 highlight offset are not BFME 2 additions: the
///       matched BFME 1 body has them too.
/// \warning The filled-box image is used without a null check, so an
///          image-look slider with no first disabled-state image crashes
///          when drawn.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - The three images are draw-data slots, so a layout chooses them; the
///   box proportions, the gap and the highlight placement are fixed.
/// - The status bit that turns scaling off is the one the parseStatus()
///   name table places after WIN_STATUS_CIRCULAR, spelled
///   WIN_STATUS_AVAIL_FLAG in BFME 2's retail table. That pairing follows
///   from the table's order and the bit this body tests; what else sets or
///   reads the bit has not been established.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The scale uses separate width and height factors, so on a screen that
///   is not 4:3 the boxes stretch unevenly.
/// - The box height multiplies the window's own height by the vertical
///   factor. On a display taller than 600 pixels the boxes are therefore
///   taller than the window and extend below it, unless the opt-out bit is
///   set.
///
/// \see W3DGadgetHorizontalSliderDraw(), \ref page_remastering_assumptions


/// \file Gadget/W3DProgressBar.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_gui_checkbox_radio_slider_draw
/// \brief W3D device layer: the progress bar's colour-look draw callback.
///
/// The file also holds two image draw routines carried over from Zero Hour
/// and BFME 1; nothing in BFME 2 is known to call them, so they are not
/// documented here. The function lexicon's entry for the image-look
/// progress bar draw points at an empty shared function in BFME 2, as in
/// BFME 1; how an image-style progress bar is drawn in BFME 2 has not been
/// established.


/// \fn void W3DGadgetProgressBarDraw(GameWindow *window, WinInstanceData *instData)
/// \brief Draw callback for colour-look progress bars: a bordered
///        background and a bar, with a light bevel, covering the current
///        percentage of the width.
///
/// The progress bar's window class calls this from its virtual draw method;
/// the retail function lexicon names it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The percentage is the window's user data, set through
///   GadgetProgressBarSetProgress() and GadgetProgressBarSystem().
/// - Picks background, background border, bar and bar border colours as
///   disabled, highlighted or enabled.
/// - Draws the background border round the whole window and the background
///   fill one pixel in, each only when its colour is defined.
/// - When the percentage is not zero, the bar's width is the window's width
///   times the percentage over 100, in integer arithmetic. The bar border
///   is drawn over that width, and the bar fill one pixel in; each is drawn
///   only when its colour is defined and the rectangle is more than one
///   pixel wide. After the fill come two bevel lines: white along the top
///   edge and light grey (200, 200, 200) down the left edge, both opaque.
///
/// The body is Zero Hour's.
///
/// \param window   The progress bar window.
/// \param instData Its instance data.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The four colours come from draw data; the bevel colours are constants in
/// this function and are drawn over the bar fill whenever the fill itself
/// is drawn (non-zero progress, a defined fill colour, and a fill more than
/// one pixel wide).
///
/// \see GadgetProgressBarSetProgress(), GadgetProgressBarSystem()


/// \file Gadget/GadgetCheckBox.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_gui_checkbox_radio_slider_draw
/// \brief The check box gadget: its input and system callbacks, and the
///        setters for its label and checked state.
///
/// A check box keeps its checked state in the selected bit of its instance
/// state, and highlights while the mouse is over it (with the mouse-track
/// style) or while it has keyboard focus. It tells its owner window about
/// clicks and focus changes by system messages, and the owning screen
/// decides what they mean. The state query, GadgetCheckBoxIsChecked(), is
/// in its own file (see \ref grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists). The file also defines
/// a toggle function from Zero Hour that is not documented here.


/// \fn WindowMsgHandledType GadgetCheckBoxInput(GameWindow *window, UnsignedInt msg, WindowMsgData mData1, WindowMsgData mData2)
/// \brief Input callback of the check box: highlights under the mouse,
///        toggles on click, Enter or Space, moves focus on Tab, and plays
///        interface sounds.
///
/// Both check box looks' window classes call this from their virtual input
/// method (see the group description).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Mouse entering and leaving: only for a window with the mouse-track
///   style, sets or clears the highlighted bit, sends the owner the
///   matching mouse-entering or mouse-leaving button message, and plays the
///   sound event Gui_ShellMapMouseOver or Gui_ShellMapMouseOut.
/// - Left drag: forwards a drag message to the owner.
/// - Left and right button down: consumed without effect.
/// - Left button up: ignored unless the highlighted bit is set (by the
///   mouse being over a mouse-track check box, or by keyboard focus); a
///   press dragged off a mouse-track box before release therefore does
///   nothing. Otherwise it flips the
///   selected bit, sends the owner the selected message with this window,
///   and plays Gui_ShellMapSelect.
/// - Right button up: if the box is checked, sends the owner the
///   right-selected message and unchecks the box; if not, the message is
///   ignored.
/// - Keys (on key-down only): Enter or Space flips the selected bit and
///   sends the selected message, without a sound. Tab moves focus to the
///   next tab stop, or to the previous one while a Shift flag is set in
///   the keyboard's modifier state. The bit tested is the one Zero Hour's
///   key-state flags assign to the left Shift key; whether BFME 2 also sets
///   it for the right Shift key has not been established. Other keys are
///   ignored, and so is every other message.
/// - The sounds go through PlaySound(), which looks the event up by name
///   in the audio system and plays it.
///
/// \param window The check box.
/// \param msg    Input message.
/// \param mData1 Message data: for key messages the key, for mouse messages
///               passed on to the owner.
/// \param mData2 For key messages the key state flags.
/// \return MSG_HANDLED for the messages above, MSG_IGNORED otherwise and
///         for the declined clicks and keys described.
///
/// \note Zero Hour plays no sounds, and moves focus with the arrow keys as
///       well as Tab (down, right and Tab forward; up and left back) with no
///       Shift test. The matched BFME 1 body already has BFME 2's
///       Tab-only handling with the Shift test, but no sounds; the three
///       sound names are BFME 2 additions (retail's string references
///       confirm them in this body).
///
/// \par Modding
/// The three event names are fixed in code; since they are looked up by
/// name, what plays is whatever the game's audio data defines under those
/// names, and changing that needs no code. The same events are used by
/// every check box of this kind.
///
/// \see GadgetCheckBoxSystem(), GadgetCheckBoxSetChecked()


/// \fn WindowMsgHandledType GadgetCheckBoxSystem(GameWindow *window, UnsignedInt msg, WindowMsgData mData1, WindowMsgData mData2)
/// \brief System callback of the check box: sets its label and tracks
///        keyboard focus.
///
/// Both check box looks' window classes call this from their virtual system
/// method. The retail function lexicon names it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Set-label: mData1 points to a UnicodeString, which becomes the
///   window's text. BFME 2 (like BFME 1) calls GameWindow's own
///   GameWindow::winSetText directly, where Zero Hour makes a virtual call,
///   so a window class that overrides text setting is bypassed here.
/// - Create and destroy: accepted, nothing to do.
/// - Input focus: mData1 says whether the window gains focus. The
///   highlighted bit follows it, the owner gets a focus-change message
///   with the flag and this window's id, and the Bool that mData2 points to
///   is set to the same flag: a check box always accepts focus. Zero Hour:
///   GameWindowManager::winSetFocus offers focus to a window and then to
///   its parents until one sets that Bool.
/// - Everything else is ignored.
///
/// \param window The check box.
/// \param msg    System message.
/// \param mData1 For set-label, a pointer to the new text; for input focus,
///               nonzero when gaining focus.
/// \param mData2 For input focus, a pointer to a Bool that receives whether
///               focus was taken. Not checked for null.
/// \return MSG_HANDLED for the four messages above, MSG_IGNORED otherwise.
///
/// \see GadgetCheckBoxSetText(), GadgetCheckBoxInput()


/// \fn void GadgetCheckBoxSetText(GameWindow *g, UnicodeString text)
/// \brief Sets a check box's label.
///
/// Sends the set-label system message with a pointer to its own copy of
/// the text, so the change goes through GadgetCheckBoxSystem(). Retail
/// callers include the window manager's check box factory, which converts
/// the layout's text label with winTextLabelToText and sets the result when
/// it is not empty (Zero Hour's GameWindowManager::gogoGadgetCheckbox does
/// the same), and setWindowText, which gives a window loaded from a layout
/// its text. Zero Hour: setWindowText first
/// looks the layout's label up in the string table, so a check box's
/// wording is a string-table entry rather than layout text.
///
/// \param g    The check box. Null is ignored.
/// \param text The new label, passed by value.
///
/// \see GadgetCheckBoxSystem(), drawCheckBoxText()


/// \fn void GadgetCheckBoxSetChecked(GameWindow *g, Bool isChecked)
/// \brief Checks or unchecks a check box from code, and notifies its owner
///        as a click would.
///
/// Sets or clears the selected bit, then sends the owner the same selected
/// message that a click sends, so the owner's handling of a click also
/// runs when a screen sets the box while filling in its controls. Retail
/// callers include the options screen
/// (AptOptions::InitGadgets and AptOptions::Reset), the player status
/// screen (AptPlayerStatus::InitGadgets) and the multiplayer game rules
/// screen.
///
/// \param g         The check box. Not checked for null.
/// \param isChecked true to check the box, false to uncheck it.
///
/// \see GadgetCheckBoxIsChecked(), GadgetCheckBoxInput()
